<GlossaryPage term="Citation Network" slug="citation-network" description="A Citation Network is the set of relevant first-party and independent references that helps people and machines verify an entity, definition, or claim." definition="A Citation Network is a connected set of source relationships in which pages, organizations, authors, and claims refer to one another with enough context to support attribution and verification.">
  <h2>Not all links are equal</h2>
  <p>A citation should answer a reader’s next question. It may be a government source for a rule, an original research paper for a technical claim, a standards body for a definition, or an independent publication for corroboration. A large list of unrelated links does not create a trustworthy network.</p>
  <h2>Build the network deliberately</h2>
  <ul><li>Link from the claim to the source at the point of support.</li><li>Prefer original and authoritative sources over summaries of summaries.</li><li>Use descriptive anchor text that names the relationship.</li><li>Keep source dates and scope visible when the claim can change.</li><li>Separate first-party assertions from independently verified facts.</li></ul>
  <p>For internal links, use the <a href="/geo/seo-to-geo/">SEO-to-GEO translation page</a>. For release review, apply the <a href="/geo-compass/anti-slop/">anti-slop standard</a>.</p>
</GlossaryPage>
